<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwig of Oettingen-Wallerstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Ludwig of Oettingen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallerstein">Wallerstein</a> was a Bavarian statesman who served King Ludwig I as interior minister from 1832 to 1837 and led the government again in 1847–1848, during the crisis over the king's affair with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lola-montez">Lola Montez</a>. He had given up the headship of his house to his brother after an unequal marriage in 1823.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-of-oettingen-wallerstein.jpg" alt="Ludwig of Oettingen-Wallerstein" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LodewijkOettingen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallerstein">Wallerstein</a> on 31 January 1791, the eldest son of Kraft Ernst, 1st Prince of Oettingen-Wallerstein, and Duchess Wilhelmine Friederike of Württemberg. He succeeded his father as ruling prince in 1802, with his mother as guardian, and in 1806 travelled with her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to ask Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> to preserve the principality's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The request failed because he would not enter French service, but in Paris he met Crown Prince Ludwig of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, who became a close friend. The principality was mediatised and divided between Bavaria and Württemberg, and from 1807 to 1810 he studied law at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Coming of age in 1810, he became Crown Court Master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and took a seat in the Council of State, and in 1813 he organised the general arming of the Oberdonaukreis. In the Württemberg estates from 1815 he helped complete that kingdom's constitution and influenced the Bavarian constitution of 1818. As head of a mediatised house he sat in the Bavarian First Chamber, where in 1819 he defended aristocratic interests against liberal tendencies.</p><p>To marry Maria Crescentia Bourgin in 1823 he gave up the headship of the house to his younger brother Friedrich, and King Maximilian I took away his court office and his seat in the chamber. When Ludwig I came to the throne these were restored, and on 14 April 1828 he was made general commissioner and regional president of the Oberdonaukreis. After his years as interior minister he served again in the crisis of 1848, and from 1849 to 1858 he sat in the Second Chamber with its liberal wing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A passionate art collector, he enlarged the Oettingen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallerstein">Wallerstein</a> collection and library, part of which Ludwig I acquired, but he spent almost all his money on art and in 1862 moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> to escape arrest at his creditors' request. After his first wife's death he married Countess Albertine Larisch von Moennich. He died at Lucerne on 22 June 1870, aged 79, and was buried beside his first wife in the Monastery of Maria Immaculata at Maihingen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis of Oettingen-Wallerstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Ludwig of Oettingen-Wallerstein</b> served as a Bavarian minister in the 1830s and 1840s, and the family still owns Harburg castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-oettingen-wallerstein">Principality of Oettingen-Wallerstein</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wallerstein, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Lucerne, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.88,10.47&amp;year=1842" aria-label="Wallerstein on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ludwig of Oettingen-Wallerstein"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5585 5898h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5585 5898h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.88,10.47&amp;year=1842">What was here in 1842? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
